Send Spinel through the USB-Serial-JTAG driver, not the console VFS

Espressif's RCP writes each HDLC chunk with write() on /dev/usbserjtag. That is
the console device: its write() returns -1 for the whole buffer whenever
usb_serial_jtag_is_connected() is false, and that monitor polls the SOF bit at
1 kHz, the same rate SOFs arrive, with a miss tolerance that is only reset on
reconnect. Every fourth isolated miss declares the host gone for one tick and the
chunk written in that window is discarded. otPlatUartSend ignores the result and
calls otPlatUartSendDone() regardless, so the frames vanish without a trace.

Queue the chunk in the driver's TX ring buffer directly instead. The ring is
sized for whole chunks in main/esp_ot_config.h.

--- a/src/port/esp_openthread_uart.c
+++ b/src/port/esp_openthread_uart.c
@@ -25,6 +25,7 @@
 #include "utils/uart.h"
 #include "driver/usb_serial_jtag_vfs.h"
 #include "driver/usb_serial_jtag.h"
+#include "freertos/FreeRTOS.h"
 #if CONFIG_OPENTHREAD_RCP_USB_SERIAL_JTAG
 #include "hal/usb_serial_jtag_ll.h"
 #endif
@@ -53,7 +54,14 @@
 
 otError otPlatUartSend(const uint8_t *buf, uint16_t buf_length)
 {
+#if CONFIG_OPENTHREAD_RCP_USB_SERIAL_JTAG
+    // Straight into the driver's ring buffer. The console VFS write() rejects the
+    // whole buffer whenever its SOF-polling connection heuristic flaps, and the
+    // SendDone below would then drop the chunk silently.
+    int rval = usb_serial_jtag_write_bytes(buf, buf_length, pdMS_TO_TICKS(100));
+#else
     int rval = write(s_uart_fd, buf, buf_length);
+#endif
 
     // DIG-727
 #if CONFIG_OPENTHREAD_RCP_USB_SERIAL_JTAG
